Description: Sound on Jam OS: an AudioManager and output stream on Jam OS's mixer (use_jamos_audio)
 Chromium on Linux plays through CRAS, PulseAudio or ALSA, none of which
 Jam OS has (args.gn turns them off), and with none a fake output drops
 every sample. This is Jam OS's back end (docs/CR-PLAN.md section 11,
 track CA1), under the GN argument use_jamos_audio (args.gn sets it), in
 media/audio/jamos with Fuchsia's pair as its model:
 - AudioManagerJamOS: one output device, the default, there when a stream
   opens on the mixer (`svc audio` in the program's list and a sound card
   behind it); preferred output 48 kHz stereo, 10 ms buffers, a source's
   own rate (8 to 192 kHz) and one or two channels kept (libjam converts);
   input stays fake (FakeAudioInputStream) until the microphone is built.
   linux/audio_manager_linux.cc makes it in place of ALSA's.
 - AudioOutputStreamJamOS: a mixer stream of float samples (libjam's
   <audio.h>: audio_open_fmt, audio_try_write, audio_position, audio_start,
   audio_pause, audio_discard), fed by a thread of its own: Start pre-rolls
   what the mixer takes when it opens the device (its lead, 171 ms) and a
   period, starts the stream, and the thread then waits on the stream's
   room event (the mixer takes a 43 ms period at a time), asking the source
   for buffers until two periods are queued, with the delay audio_position
   gives; Stop wakes and joins it and throws the queue away (pause, then
   discard), so a Start after it goes on with the source's next frames,
   none twice. The volume scales the samples, as Fuchsia's does.
 - JamOutput (jam_output.cc), the only file that includes libjam's headers
   (the sysroot's include/jamos/libos: their OK and ERR_* are macros):
   past the four streams the mixer gives one opener (libjam keeps one a
   process), streams open on openers of its own (audio_open_on), as the
   audio service holds every page's stream in one process.
Forwarded: not-needed (Jam OS's own sound output)
